## Design Decisions
I decided to transition wire routing from an internal mid-plate corner cutout to a top-edge vertical slot cut directly into the external side wall. Because the COB LED aluminum channels (15 × 9 mm cross-section) have pre-attached, fragile solder joints, forcing the wires through a 90-degree internal mid-plate bend would introduce severe bending stress and risk shearing the solder pads. Routing directly through an external slot allows the light bars to drop in flat with zero wire strain.

I also decided to keep the 5 mm mid-plate completely continuous and solid around the lighting channels rather than introducing large cutouts. A solid divider maximizes structural stiffness during gel contact, prevents lower-chamber ambient light leaks, and ensures that the upper optical chamber remains an isolated dark box.

For manufacturing handover, I opted to supply STEP (B-Rep) files instead of STL or 3MF. STEP files retain exact mathematical curve definitions for circular holes (Φ3.2 mm bolt holes and Φ26 mm lens aperture), eliminating polygon faceting and ensuring reliable clearance fits without manual post-machining.

---

## Observations
In FDM additive manufacturing, small internal cylindrical holes consistently experience dimensional undersizing (typically shrinking by 0.2 to 0.4 mm) due to polymer thermal contraction and toolpath curvature pull. Setting M2.5 clearance holes to nominal Φ3.2 mm provides an ideal sliding clearance of approximately 2.9 to 3.0 mm on the finished part, allowing easy optical alignment.

COB LED aluminum strips concentrate mechanical vulnerability entirely at their terminal wire pads. Physical routing geometry must be designed around these pre-existing wire exit vectors rather than relying on field modification or high-stress tight-radius bends.

Standard 2-perimeter shell thicknesses in dark filament exhibit microscopic optical bleed when exposed to high-intensity ambient lighting. A minimum wall perimeter setting of 4 passes (≥1.6 mm solid boundary) is required to guarantee complete optical opacity for photometric stereo imaging.

